KNOWNBUG
main.cpp
--cpp20
^\[main\.assertion\.1\] line \d+ member erase\(iterator->const_iterator\) in template body: SUCCESS$
^VERIFICATION SUCCESSFUL$
^EXIT=0$
^SIGNAL=0$
--
^.*found no match for symbol.*$
^CONVERSION ERROR$
--
KNOWNBUG: a user-defined conversion (iterator -> const_iterator) via a
converting constructor template whose parameter is a class-template-id
(Iter<Q, C>) is not applied during member-call overload resolution inside an
instantiated function template body.  The overloaded member erase() is found
but its candidate is rejected before the argument's user-defined conversion is
considered, so the call is dropped and the assertion fails (n stays 3).

Direct initialization (`const_iterator c = it;`) resolves the same conversion
correctly, so the converting constructor itself works; the gap is specifically
in member-call overload resolution in this context.  This is the header-free
analogue of std::erase_if's `__cont.erase(iterator, iterator)` (see
cpp20_vector_erase_if).  Reclassify CORE once the conversion is applied.
